          "Justification",
          "According to the INFORM Severity Index methodology, the sum of people in levels 3, 4 and 5 is equal to the total number of people in need. To estimate the severity distribution of the people in need ACAPS used the PIN from the HNRP 2026 (level 2 and level 3). The reliability of this data is medium.",
          "This figure (1,167,337) corresponds to the number of people facing IPC Phase 3 according to the 2022 IPC projection and remains the latest available disaggregated estimate. FEWS NET\u2019s October 2024 analysis and June 2025 update confirm continued high levels of food insecurity in the same provinces, citing failed harvests, price shocks, and reduced coping capacity, which supports the plausibility of ongoing Phase 3 conditions. However, no updated IPC or equivalent severity analysis with revised population figures is available. Given the reliance on an outdated IPC baseline, reliability is considered Low.",
          "The 2025 Humanitarian Response Plan identifies 500,000 people in Burundi as being in Severity Level 3 (Serious). This group faces significant unmet needs resulting from recurrent floods and droughts, reduced agricultural production, and limited access to essential services. While their needs are less acute than those in Levels 4\u20135, they remain highly vulnerable to deterioration without sustained recovery and development support. The figure comes from the HRP\u2019s aggregated severity analysis, which combines sectoral assessments and inter-agency data to classify needs across the country.",
          "According to INFORM Severity Index methodology, the sum of people in levels 3, 4 and 5 is equal to the total number of people in need. To estimate the severity distribution of the people in need ACAPS  assigned the total number of people in need to level 3, leaving an info-gap for level 4 and level 5 since there is no other reliable source for the severity distribution for each level. Therefore the number of people in level 3 includes all the refugees and asylum seekers. "
