          "Justification",
          "In 2026, the entire population of Afghanistan remain affected by a complex, overlapping crisis driven by prolonged economic collapse, climate-related shocks, widespread food insecurity, and large-scale population movements. Recurrent droughts and floods continue to undermine agriculture and livelihoods, while limited access to basic services weakens household coping capacities nationwide. Severe restrictions on women\u2019s participation in education and employment disproportionately affect families and communities. At the same time, significant humanitarian funding shortfalls reduce the availability of life-saving assistance. The continued return of large numbers of Afghans from Pakistan and Iran further strains already overstretched services. Although needs vary in intensity, but the whole population remain affected.\nThis figure is taken from OCHA HNRP 2026 since it is the most up-to-date and multi sectoral assessment for 2026 humanitarian situation in Afghanistan. The reliability of this data is high because it is aligned with coordinated response in the country.",
          "The affected population (2,267,300) equals IPC Phases 2\u20135 from the 2022 IPC analysis in Cunene, Hu\u00edla, Namibe, Cuando Cubango and Moxico. FEWS NET (Oct 2024; Jun 2025) indicates continued consumption gaps and negative coping linked to erratic rainfall, poor harvests and high prices, supporting the continued relevance of Phase 2+ outcomes, but it does not provide updated phase totals. Reliability is Low due to the age of the IPC estimates.",
          "The number of people affected corresponds to the total population of the country/living in the areas concerned by climatic shocks, which entail  floods, drought/drier conditions. The entire country has been categorised as impacted.\n\nFlooding, driven by the El Ni\u00f1o and Indian Ocean Dipole phenomena during the September 2023\u2013May 2024 rainy season, affected & continues to impact multiple provinces across Burundi. The destruction of homes, agricultural land, and key infrastructure has disrupted livelihoods and local markets, reducing household income and food availability. These climatic shocks compound existing socio-economic fragility, including high poverty rates, limited access to basic services, and dependence on rain-fed agriculture. ",
