          "Justification",
          "Displacement in Afghanistan remains frequent and unpredictable, involving both internal movement and cross-border returns from countries such as Iran, Pakistan. This is driven by a combination of violence, climate hazards, along with deportation efforts in refugee-hosting countries. The total number of people displaced by the crisis includes: 3,206,392 internally displaced persons (IDPs), 2,917500 returnees in 2025, 278,200 returnees in January & February 2026, 3738500 Afghans in Iran as of February 2026, and 1750300 Afghans in Pakistan as of February 2026. According to the UNHCR, the total Afghan population in Iran is 4.4 million as of 17 August 2025 and between 18 August to 28 February 2026 a total of 661500 individuals returned from Iran. So, the  of Afghans residing in Iran as of 28 February would be (4400000 - 661500) = 3738500. Similarly, the total Afghan population in Pakistan is 1.98 million as of January 2026, while 169700 individuals returned in January and February 2026, so the current number of Afghans in Pakistan would be (1.98 - 169700)= 1810300. The IDPs, returnees, and Afghans in Iran and Pakistan are taken from the UNHCR data portal. Lastly more than 115,000 Afghans have fled their homes near the border as heavy airstrikes and shelling have resumed between Pakistan and Afghanistan. \n\nDue to constant changes in people\u2019s displacement status, these sources are currently the most consistent and regularly updated. The overall reliability of the displacement figure is considered medium, as the total is likely an underestimation and continuous movement is not updated as of current period.",
          "Reliable and recent data on internal displacement attributable to climate-related hazards in Angola remain limited. While some climate-related displacement has been reported, available event-level information indicates that recent movements are primarily associated with flooding, rather than drought, and cannot be clearly attributed to the crisis drivers under analysis. In addition, existing estimates lack sufficient geographic disaggregation for key affected provinces (e.g. Cuando Cubango, Huambo and Lunda-Sul) and are based on displacement events recorded in 2024, making them outdated for the current update. Given these limitations in timeliness, driver attribution and spatial specificity, this indicator is left as an information gap",
          "The number of people displaced by climatic events between January and February including 1,453 displaced between 18\u201331 January 2026, and 1,953 between 1\u20137 February 2026 according to the latest IOM figures highlighting the continued and significant impact of climatic shocks on communities. The reliability is assessed as high the displacement is a result of the recent hailtorms, strong winds and torrential rains.",
          "This number corresponds to the number of refugees and asylum seekers  from Eastern DRC in Burundi. Following the large-scale arrival of Congolese refugees into Burundi, fleeing intensified clashes in South Kivu particularly in and around Kamanyola, Luvungi, Katogota, and Uvira the humanitarian situation has further deteriorated. Since 5 December, renewed confrontations have triggered a significant new influx of people seeking safety across the border.\nThis latest wave of displacement comes in addition to earlier arrivals recorded throughout the year. Since January 2025, more than 70,000 people fleeing the Democratic Republic of Congo have arrived in Burundi in need of international refugee protection and humanitarian assistance. The December influx has further increased overall displacement from the DRC into Burundi, bringing the total number of Congolese refugees in the country to 179,319. These figures are taken from UNHCR. UNHCR is used here as it is the most reliable  and provides monthly updates on the number of refugees in Burundi. The reliability of this indicator is medium as  the methodologies used for data collection are generally consistent, though minor variations exist that could affect accuracy."
